WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.020
<v Riley>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got heart scans that only matter for the borderline cases, solar panels boosting turnip and pepper yields by triple digits, and a federal exemption that could let a pipeline bypass protections for salmon runs already found to interfere with killer whale survival. That and more, coming right up.

00:00:33.495 --> 00:00:51.505
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Sunday, September 13th, 2026. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations. Today's theme is Science, Wonder &amp; the Natural World.

00:00:52.298 --> 00:01:06.570
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today's stories run from heart scans to prairie grass in England to a pipeline fight that touches killer whales — the thread running through it is what happens when evidence gets acted on, and what happens when it doesn't.

00:01:06.808 --> 00:01:26.517
<v Casey>Quick weather check — out at Horsefly Lake this morning, mainly clear and around 6 degrees, with a high of 19 and overcast moving in through the day. 100 Mile House and Williams Lake both around 7 with clear skies, Quesnel around 6 and cloudy, Tatla Lake around 7 and overcast. No precipitation expected anywhere in the region.

00:01:26.755 --> 00:01:28.115
<v Riley>Let's get into it.

00:01:29.515 --> 00:01:43.621
<v Riley>Three close-to-home stories today, and then a real cluster on the science side. Starting local: the nomination period has closed for the Williams Lake municipal election, and the field is set. Two candidates for mayor, fifteen for council.

00:01:43.881 --> 00:01:45.738
<v Casey>Fifteen for how many seats?

00:01:45.997 --> 00:01:58.619
<v Riley>Six council seats. That's nearly three candidates per chair, a more contested field than the last cycle. The Tribune has the full candidate list up now, and we'll keep tracking it through the fall.

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:18.221
<v Casey>Provincial politics had its own shuffle this week. Former B.C. Conservative MLA Teresa Wat is joining Peter Milobar's new party. She's the sixth sitting MLA to make that move since the split began — the group, co-founded by Milobar and Ian Paton, is now the third-largest caucus in the legislature.

00:02:18.481 --> 00:02:25.534
<v Riley>Six defections in one legislative session is a real number — that's close to a caucus's worth of turnover.

00:02:25.794 --> 00:02:39.158
<v Casey>It is, and it changes the seat math in Victoria in ways still shaking out. No local MLA has switched allegiance yet, but the pressure on smaller parties to consolidate before the next election is building.

00:02:39.418 --> 00:02:49.442
<v Riley>Worth remembering Milobar himself represents Kamloops-area interests, so a Cariboo voter isn't watching from the sidelines. The regional caucus math touches forestry policy and health funding formulas.

00:02:50.481 --> 00:03:03.474
<v Casey>And this weekend marked the 25th anniversary of September 11th, with Canadians and Americans gathering at the B.C. border to remember. A bugler played the Last Post at the ceremony, according to the Tribune's coverage.

00:03:03.734 --> 00:03:07.075
<v Riley>A quarter century. Worth sitting with for a second.

00:03:08.115 --> 00:03:28.162
<v Casey>Now to the science stories, and it opens with something a lot of us have had recommended by a doctor at some point — the heart calcium scan. A ten-year Northwestern study, tracking more than six thousand adults, found the scans add little for people already clearly low-risk or clearly high-risk by standard measures.

00:03:28.421 --> 00:03:31.391
<v Riley>So where do they actually earn their keep?

00:03:31.651 --> 00:03:50.584
<v Casey>The borderline cases. If your usual blood pressure, cholesterol, and family history calculation puts you in that uncertain middle zone, the scan gives real added information about whether you need treatment. It nudged the model's prediction accuracy from 0.73 up to 0.75 — modest overall, but meaningful for that group specifically.

00:03:50.844 --> 00:04:03.095
<v Riley>That's useful triage guidance for a region where getting to a scanner often means a drive to Kamloops or Prince George. Fewer unnecessary trips if it's only worth doing for the ambiguous cases.

00:04:04.134 --> 00:04:16.385
<v Casey>Staying with growing things — researchers at Kew's Wakehurst site in Sussex have planted swathes of American prairie species, testing which handle drought best as the British climate keeps getting hotter and drier.

00:04:16.645 --> 00:04:35.578
<v Riley>Prairie grasses evolved for that kind of moisture stress — deep root systems, dormancy strategies built for landscapes that go bone dry every summer. Might be less about importing prairie species here and more about not paving over the native grassland cousins we already have around Riske Creek and the Chilcotin.

00:04:36.617 --> 00:04:54.808
<v Casey>Sticking with practical questions people rarely get a straight answer on — Engadget has a piece on how to actually tell when an EV battery needs replacing. Short version: it's not about the odometer, it's about behavior — sudden range drops, slower charging, warning lights that weren't there before.

00:04:55.068 --> 00:05:06.576
<v Riley>Good to know before the next vehicle purchase around here leans electric, and useful for anyone already driving on these roads where range anxiety on a Highway 20 trip is real.

00:05:07.616 --> 00:05:19.495
<v Casey>Closer to policy — the Fraser Institute's latest report says B.C. now has the highest share of students enrolled in independent schools of any province in Canada, and that enrollment keeps climbing.

00:05:19.755 --> 00:05:33.120
<v Riley>Rural areas barely register in that trend, though. Independent schools cluster in the Lower Mainland and Victoria — the Cariboo doesn't have the population density to support many private options even where parents might want them.

00:05:33.379 --> 00:05:44.888
<v Casey>True. More a Lower Mainland story wearing a provincial headline. Though it raises a fair question about public school funding formulas, since enrollment shifts anywhere affect how per-student dollars get allocated.

00:05:45.927 --> 00:05:58.178
<v Riley>Over in Scotland, researchers are testing agrivoltaics on turnip fields — solar panels mounted above the crop on raised structures rather than instead of it. In the best-performing setups, yields actually went up.

00:05:58.438 --> 00:05:59.552
<v Casey>By how much?

00:05:59.811 --> 00:06:15.775
<v Riley>The broader study, looking at multiple crop pairings, found some panel-and-pepper combinations boosting yield by well over a hundred percent, with the panels generating two to five times more energy than the farm needs. Turnips got a measurable boost too, per Forbes' write-up.

00:06:16.034 --> 00:06:35.710
<v Casey>That could make sense on ranch land here with open exposure — shade for stock in July, power for the operation, a cash crop underneath the array. It'd need capital most small operations don't have lying around, though a co-op splitting the panel cost across a few ranches might make the math work.

00:06:36.749 --> 00:06:46.030
<v Riley>Broccoli sprouts from an Idaho grower have been recalled after a salmonella outbreak linked to cases in four American states, with two hospitalizations so far.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:55.200
<v Casey>Worth checking your fridge even up here — a lot of our fresh produce runs through the same U.S. distributors this time of year.

00:06:56.239 --> 00:07:13.687
<v Riley>Last one in this cluster is strange, and a little moving. Researchers gave direct cash payments to low-income mothers and then measured biological aging markers in their babies afterward. The payments appear to have slowed the pace of aging in the infants compared to a control group.

00:07:13.947 --> 00:07:32.880
<v Casey>ScienceAlert's write-up doesn't give a mechanism, just the outcome — so there's a gap between "cash changed the biology" and knowing exactly how. Still, the idea that poverty acts as a biological stressor on child development isn't new as a hypothesis; seeing it show up in actual aging biomarkers is different.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:52.853
<v Riley>A few more before we wrap. South of the border, ICE seized more than twenty-two thousand ancient artifacts in Florida last month — coins, terracotta, glass vessels, jewelry — the agency's largest object recovery on record, alongside reporting that the Department of Homeland Security has waived cultural preservation safeguards more broadly.

00:07:53.892 --> 00:08:06.885
<v Casey>A U.S. Fifth Circuit Court ruling found residents don't have a constitutional right to safe drinking water, upholding the dismissal of a lawsuit against the city of Jackson, Mississippi, over years of lead-contaminated tap water.

00:08:07.145 --> 00:08:16.426
<v Riley>That one has teeth for infrastructure funding fights generally — it sets a legal ceiling on what residents anywhere can force a city to guarantee.

00:08:17.466 --> 00:08:30.088
<v Casey>Up in Alaska, a startup called Sitkana is testing underwater tidal turbines that use idle fishing vessels through the winter months to help power isolated coastal communities, while giving boat owners extra off-season income.

00:08:31.127 --> 00:08:51.174
<v Riley>And closer to an industry a lot of Cariboo families work in directly — the BC Forest Safety Council's Resource Road Driver Training program hit a participation milestone this week. It's a two-day in-field course teaching decision-making for driving logging roads, a skill set that doesn't show up on a standard driver's license test.

00:08:51.434 --> 00:09:01.086
<v Casey>Good, unglamorous infrastructure. Nobody thinks about it until someone's missing that training on a washed-out grade in April with a loaded truck coming the other way.

00:09:02.125 --> 00:09:12.149
<v Riley>One more before the spotlight. Fox News Media has parted ways with anchor Maria Bartiromo, after she reportedly shared internal Fox Business communications with the White House.

00:09:12.409 --> 00:09:20.947
<v Casey>Not a story we get a vote on up here, but a data point on how newsroom-to-government pipelines get treated once they surface.

00:09:21.727 --> 00:09:24.697
<v Riley>On that note, let's get into today's spotlight.

00:09:26.097 --> 00:10:05.104
<v Riley>Today is National Hunting Day, and that's a good moment to talk about the Cariboo Chilcotin Conservation Society. They've been running environmental education programs since 1996 — Water Wise, Waste Wise, Skates for All, and field programs that bring local kids out onto the land instead of just reading about it in a textbook. Ethical hunting has always been part of this region's relationship with the land, and their programs connect that heritage to responsible stewardship for a new generation. You can find them at conservationsociety.ca.

00:10:05.422 --> 00:10:19.936
<v Casey>Twenty-nine years running on a shoestring nonprofit budget is its own kind of conservation success story — and it's a fitting handoff into what's actually protected, and how, in today's deep dive.

00:10:21.337 --> 00:10:43.926
<v Riley>Ottawa is moving to let major infrastructure projects bypass the Species at Risk Act, if they're designated projects of "national interest" under the new Building Canada Act. Environmental groups are running ad campaigns about Southern Resident killer whales. This isn't just activist messaging — the U.S. Marine Mammal Commission, an independent federal agency, has raised its own formal concerns.

00:10:44.194 --> 00:10:51.468
<v Casey>Cross-border concern from a government body. That's a different category than an op-ed writer with an axe to grind.

00:10:51.736 --> 00:11:06.285
<v Riley>The case in question is the Roberts Bank Terminal 2 expansion in the Fraser estuary, plus a proposed Alberta pipeline feeding into the same corridor. Federal environmental assessments already found the terminal infrastructure interferes with Chinook salmon migration.

00:11:06.553 --> 00:11:09.233
<v Casey>Which is the whales' primary food source.

00:11:09.501 --> 00:11:21.370
<v Riley>So the real question isn't abstract — whether removing the review requirement is a genuine tradeoff between progress and nature, or a way of overriding a finding that's already settled science.

00:11:21.638 --> 00:11:38.484
<v Casey>What stands out about the mechanism is that the whole point of exempting a project from species-at-risk review is to avoid the friction that review would create. Nobody designs mitigation around a finding once the project's declared exempt from having to answer to it.

00:11:38.752 --> 00:11:54.832
<v Riley>That's not automatically the wrong call, though. Mitigation technology exists — staged construction windows timed around salmon runs, acoustic dampening for vessel traffic, monitoring buoys that trigger slowdowns the moment whales are detected. Engineering can reduce harm without stopping the project outright.

00:11:55.101 --> 00:12:02.375
<v Casey>If anyone's required to use it. That's the whole argument for the exemption existing — so nobody has to.

00:12:02.643 --> 00:12:20.638
<v Riley>Reasonable to be wary of that. But mitigation only gets designed and funded when someone's forced to sit with a finding long enough to solve it. Remove the requirement, and you don't just cut paperwork — you remove the incentive to engineer around the problem at all.

00:12:20.906 --> 00:12:45.026
<v Casey>That's the shape of the real question underneath this whole decision. Every friction point in a process like environmental review is also the mechanism that produced the practice — the studies, the staged construction windows, the monitoring stations. Make it easier to build, and you don't just save time. You lose the muscle that only ever got built because the friction demanded it.

00:12:45.294 --> 00:12:57.546
<v Riley>Southern Resident killer whales are down to a few dozen individuals total. That's not a group that recovers from a policy experiment gone wrong. There's no margin left for trial and error.

00:12:57.814 --> 00:13:13.894
<v Casey>And Chinook salmon runs on the Fraser system pass close enough to Cariboo territory that this isn't a coastal abstraction for us. Those fish move through waters this region depends on long before they reach the whales waiting at the river mouth.

00:13:14.698 --> 00:13:32.310
<v Riley>That's the piece that gets lost when a Vancouver op-ed frames this as purely a coastal issue. The Fraser is one watershed, start to finish. What happens at Roberts Bank touches the same system that Cariboo Nations have monitored and depended on for generations further upstream.

00:13:32.578 --> 00:13:42.916
<v Casey>If federal assessments already found interference with Chinook migration, and that gets waved through anyway, the exemption isn't bypassing an unknown risk. It's bypassing a documented finding.

00:13:43.184 --> 00:13:54.287
<v Riley>This isn't "we don't have enough science yet, let's proceed cautiously." The assessment exists. The finding exists. The exemption is built so that finding doesn't have to be answered.

00:13:54.555 --> 00:14:06.807
<v Casey>Which makes this a different animal than a normal cost-benefit tradeoff. A tradeoff implies weighing two real things against each other honestly. This is one side declaring itself outside the weighing altogether.

00:14:07.075 --> 00:14:23.155
<v Riley>National interest designations aren't new for major infrastructure — highways and past pipelines have used a version of this mechanism before. What's new is applying it where a species-at-risk finding is this concrete and this close to an already critically endangered population.

00:14:23.423 --> 00:14:32.612
<v Casey>Fine, the tool's not new. The application right now is the test case, with an endangered population as the actual stakes on the table.

00:14:33.416 --> 00:14:56.005
<v Riley>Two other science stories this week point at something related, if you look sideways at them. The centenarian-offspring study found children of people who lived to a hundred have dramatically lower cardiovascular risk than their peers — but disease onset was delayed, not eliminated. And those same offspring also tend to eat measurably better diets than the general population.

00:14:56.273 --> 00:15:03.165
<v Casey>Which means you can't cleanly separate the genetics from the environment those genetics happened to grow up inside.

00:15:03.433 --> 00:15:15.301
<v Riley>Right, and press coverage reaches for the genetic explanation because it's tidier. Easier to say "it's in your DNA" than untangle decades of household habits, income stability, and access to care.

00:15:15.569 --> 00:15:28.970
<v Casey>Same instinct as the pipeline exemption, in a way. The simpler explanation, or the simpler process, gets chosen not because it's more accurate but because it needs less time sitting with the complexity underneath it.

00:15:29.238 --> 00:15:47.998
<v Riley>The Type 1 diabetes finding has a similar shape. Splitting the diagnosis into two genetic subtypes — researchers are calling them T1DE1 and T1DE2 — is a genuine scientific advance. That kind of split is usually the step before more targeted treatment, the way cancer subtyping was decades ago.

00:15:48.266 --> 00:16:05.878
<v Casey>"Eventually" is doing the work in that sentence. A genome-wide association study identifying two endotypes is not a new treatment reaching a clinic in the Cariboo. The gap between a finding like this and a changed standard of care has historically run a decade or more.

00:16:06.146 --> 00:16:21.461
<v Riley>Sure, but the alternative to funding that frontier research is staying with one undifferentiated diagnosis forever, treating two distinct diseases as if they were the same one. Some of this work has to happen before the applied version ever exists.

00:16:21.729 --> 00:16:37.043
<v Casey>Nobody in Quesnel gets a different diabetes treatment protocol because a genomics paper published last week. I'm not against the research getting funded — I'm against headlines that make it sound like tomorrow's patients get something different because of it.

00:16:37.847 --> 00:16:50.099
<v Riley>Coming back to the pipeline case, since that's the one with an actual decision point in front of it, not a decade out — what would you want to see happen procedurally?

00:16:50.367 --> 00:17:08.362
<v Casey>At minimum, the exemption shouldn't apply anywhere a federal assessment has already produced a specific finding of harm. If the Roberts Bank data already shows salmon migration interference, that's not a knowledge gap to work around later. That's the exact finding the exemption is built to erase.

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:25.859
<v Riley>I'd add monitoring requirements that survive the exemption even if the formal review process doesn't — acoustic buoys, salmon counts, whale population tracking, published on a quarterly cycle instead of buried in a five-hundred-page environmental impact statement nobody outside the ministry reads for five years.

00:17:26.127 --> 00:17:40.293
<v Casey>Who enforces that once the legal requirement to conduct the review is gone? Strip the Species at Risk Act's teeth out of a project, and you also remove whoever was legally accountable for checking compliance against it.

00:17:40.561 --> 00:17:57.407
<v Riley>Don't have a clean answer to that one. Maybe it falls to Fisheries and Oceans Canada's existing salmon monitoring mandate, since that program exists independent of species-at-risk review. The infrastructure for tracking is already there — it just wouldn't carry the same legal force.

00:17:57.675 --> 00:18:11.841
<v Casey>Which is thinner protection than what exists today, by definition. We'd be trading a legal requirement for a discretionary one, and discretionary requirements are the first thing cut when a project's budget gets tight in year three.

00:18:12.109 --> 00:18:16.704
<v Riley>And the whales don't exactly get to file the public comment themselves.

00:18:16.972 --> 00:18:20.800
<v Casey>No, they don't. Somebody has to do it for them.

00:18:21.604 --> 00:18:37.685
<v Riley>Nobody up here gets a direct vote on the Roberts Bank decision, but the watershed argument means it's not someone else's fight either. Fisheries and Oceans Canada takes public comment on major project reviews, worth checking whether this file is still open.

00:18:37.953 --> 00:19:05.519
<v Casey>And if you want the raw data instead of the op-ed version, the federal environmental assessment for Roberts Bank Terminal 2 is public record. Dense reading, but it's the actual finding everyone's arguing about. If the salmon show up healthy at the top of the watershed one of these falls, we'll be the first to say the exemption worked out fine — though history suggests don't hold your breath on that one.

00:19:05.787 --> 00:19:13.061
<v Riley>Fair enough. If you've got thoughts on any of this, feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a reaches us directly.

00:19:13.329 --> 00:19:16.010
<v Casey>Have a good rest of your Sunday.

00:19:16.278 --> 00:19:19.340
<v Riley>Watch the whales' back for us out there.